nd{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 & 12 \\\\ 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}36 & -42 \\\\ -42 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+81+(-16)=101.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "557c85c44ab4f4a5c9e6fd9aff7bd7ea4eb3fa6b21ed701e6ca8762b6b287ce2"} {"question" "q-137887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "101"] ["math" "116"] ["math" "135"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "88"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "101"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $17a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 \\\\ -34 & 68\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ -34 & 68 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n17a+\\frac{b}{c}=17(\\frac{3}{17})+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "557c85c44ab4f4a5c9e6fd9aff7bd7ea4eb3fa6b21ed701e6ca8762b6b287ce2"} {"question" "q-158458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-17"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{17}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ -8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 100\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 100\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 100 & 100\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 & 50 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "557c85c44ab4f4a5c9e6fd9aff7bd7ea4eb3fa6b21ed701e6ca8762b6b287ce2"} {"question" "q-137926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14\\cdot 9-9\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "557c85c44ab4f4a5c9e6fd9aff7bd7ea4eb3fa6b21ed701e6ca8762b6b287ce2"} {"question" "q-158372" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158372" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-158372" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "557c85c44ab4f4a5c9e6fd9aff7bd7ea4eb3fa6b21ed701e6ca8762b6b287ce2"} {"question" "q-137917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 30 & 20 & 40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{3})}{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "557c85c44ab4f4a5c9e6fd9aff7bd7ea4eb3fa6b21ed701e6ca8762b6b287ce2"} {"question" "q-137920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "q-137920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/state.json" "file_sha256" "0100180f1c8e9bf86ca27d2d8d13f44acb386c03cf1b9cf000429bcefd5e370c"} {"question" "e-9831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/bdc283cb936da0169970929ff4f078cc4b1ad58ae124625655e7f8d3a0a15b24.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/c149ecd1fe76187859c289443581b4b2999aa2bd1e409723253a33bee56fb100.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/example-9831.json" "file_sha256" "46bfbf8c43a1e32c04be020da22f681e7921767bd374e93f2dc9739b8834c963"} {"question" "e-9831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/f7cc4573dd79ddd8b872f75bae85670e72dcdcba0b2a514d9e21a27c599fc711.png)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}29 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/example-9831.json" "file_sha256" "46bfbf8c43a1e32c04be020da22f681e7921767bd374e93f2dc9739b8834c963"} {"question" "e-9832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/2429c49383a8610e76edcee474205d1f261fc81d1ec65ac4178ebc60f3c6d8d8.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/example-9832.json" "file_sha256" "7d47908e4787ce3f1fa99e896c7f687f9aafa51d0d18a285c6e486f8a08198e2"} {"question" "e-9832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/6607f2839cfc50abcf9a25f0cd22319042c29009fec90ffef35e43d8f24b5be7.png)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/c7ad0d1ea0ea307fe96ce69b9e4f046d9eab66a56bbba8de7105a3ad7e150be9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/example-9832.json" "file_sha256" "7d47908e4787ce3f1fa99e896c7f687f9aafa51d0d18a285c6e486f8a08198e2"}] "usage" {"e-9832" [] "e-9831" []} "basis" 2704 "retractions" [[17592186161837 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?"] [17592186161837 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwe pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(WA)^{T} \\cdot WAx &= (WA)^{T} \\cdot Wb \\\\\nA^{T}(W^{T}W)Ax &= A^{T}(W^{T}W)b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}29 \\\\ 54\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104\n$$"] [17592186161838 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}$."] [17592186161838 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwe pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(WA)^{T} \\cdot WAx &= (WA)^{T} \\cdot Wb \\\\\nA^{T}(W^{T}W)Ax &= A^{T}(W^{T}W)b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{13}{7} + x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
